course: dictionary
lesson: 11380
title: Abruptly: sudden or short
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Abruptly is an adverb, so it tells you how something happened. Most often it means suddenly and with no warning: «The music stopped abruptly.» Nobody was ready for the silence, because it came all at once.
explain: The word can also show how someone speaks or acts. «"Leave me alone," he said abruptly.» Here abruptly means in a short, rude way, with no kind words first. The clues around the word tell you which meaning fits.
explain: Use the swap check. Put "suddenly" into the sentence. If it still makes sense, the first meaning fits. If it sounds wrong, try "shortly and rudely" instead.
fact: «Abruptly» means suddenly and unexpectedly.
fact: «Abruptly» can also mean in a short, rude or unfriendly way.
fact: In science, a graph that changes abruptly has a sharp jump instead of a smooth line.
fact: «Abruptly» is an adverb, so it tells you how an action happens.
exercise: «The music stopped abruptly, and the room went silent.» What does abruptly mean here? | *Suddenly and unexpectedly | Again and again | Slowly and gently | Very loudly
exercise: «"Go away," she said abruptly, without looking up.» What does abruptly suggest? | In a soft whisper | In a happy, friendly way | *In a short, rude way | After a long talk
exercise: Find and copy the adverb in this sentence: «The lamp flickered abruptly and went out.» | lamp | the | *abruptly | and
test: «The bus braked abruptly when the ball rolled into the road.» What does abruptly mean here? | *Suddenly, with no warning | Quietly, so no one heard | Late, after everyone was ready | Slowly and carefully
test: In a graph, what does it mean when a line changes abruptly? | It stays flat for a long time | It goes up in a smooth curve | It disappears from the page | *It jumps sharply, with no smooth join
exam: «He ended the phone call abruptly, before I could say goodbye.» What does abruptly mean here? | *Suddenly, with no warning | Politely, with a long goodbye | Quietly, in a whisper | Happily, with a big smile
